{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '48,175p'; cat claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"    48\tPairwise judgments are a natural interface for evaluation: a voter chooses between responses, or a game produces a winner and a loser. A ranking model turns these local outcomes into global scores. Such scores are useful precisely because they reduce a complex comparison graph to a compact summary. That compression also makes it difficult to tell which observations sustain a displayed ordering. Sampling uncertainty, dependence between observations, and targeted changes to the observed sample need not affect that ordering in the same way.\n    49\t\n    50\tWe ask a deliberately finite-sample question: \\emph{how much does a fitted ranking change when a small, explicitly constrained subset of its observed comparisons is removed?} This is not an assessment of the true quality of a model or team. The reference is the ranking fitted before deletion, not an external ground-truth ordering. A large change indicates sensitivity of that fitted summary; a small change under a particular rule does not certify robustness to every equally sized subset.\n    51\t\n    52\tOur audit couples a local influence calculation with bounded exact-refit selection. For each possible challenger to the clean leader, influence values define an immutable ordering of rows. Deletions must be nested prefixes of that ordering. A screening calculation reduces the contenders considered by retained-data fits, while an independent evaluator checks the clean-fit certificate and refits each deleted dataset. This separation matters: an internally predicted rank reversal is not itself a measured ranking change.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe empirical scope is crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences and historical basketball outcomes, fitted with the same static Bradley--Terry objective. These settings provide contrasting sources of pairwise data without constituting a controlled intervention on evaluation design. We find greater aggregate disruption than the measured baselines, but the ordering of deletion rules depends on dataset and budget. Conditional resampling makes that dependence especially clear: the high-loss comparator is consistently more disruptive in the stored Arena resamples and less disruptive in the NBA resamples.\n    55\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    56\t\n    57\tThe contributions are therefore narrow and verifiable: a source-inspectable frozen-prefix audit with independent refitting; a budget-resolved comparison against high-loss and random deletion on contrasting real-data settings; and an uncertainty analysis that explicitly separates repeated random masks from fixed-mask data reweighting. We do not identify a globally most damaging deletion set, isolate the causal contribution of shortlist refitting, or attribute the cross-setting differences to crowdsourcing rather than other dataset properties.\n    58\t\n    59\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    60\t  \\centering\n    61\t  \\includegraphics[width=0.98\\textwidth]{figures/audit_overview.png}\n    62\t  \\caption{A common audit for contrasting pairwise-data settings. A regularized fit supplies clean influence orders; bounded contender selection uses retained-data refits without changing those orders. Independent refits measure disruption. The uncertainty branch reweights provenance groups with deletion masks fixed: it does not repeat contender selection.}\n    63\t  \\label{fig:overview}\n    64\t  % @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L145]]\n    65\t  % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L831]]\n    66\t\\end{figure}\n    67\t\n    68\t\\section{Related Work}\n    69\t\\paragraph{Pairwise ranking.}\n    70\tProbabilistic ranking has classical roots in tournament likelihoods and comparative judgment \\citep{Zermelo1929,Thurstone1927}. Bradley--Terry modeling \\citep{Bradley1952} and the binary-comparison ranking problem \\citep{Ford1957} provide the immediate foundation. Generalized Bradley--Terry estimation and software support richer fitting procedures \\citep{Hunter2004,Turner2012}; explicit tie models \\citep{Davidson1970} and bias-reduced likelihood \\citep{Firth1993} are distinct from the half-win ties and quadratic penalty used here. Models over permutations \\citep{Plackett1975} address a related but different observation space. Rank Centrality and counting-based ranking study estimation and recovery from pairwise comparisons \\citep{Negahban2012,Shah2017}. Our endpoint instead measures changes relative to an already fitted ordering, using a normalized inversion count related to rank-correlation statistics \\citep{Kendall1938}.\n    71\t\n    72\t\\paragraph{Influence and sample removal.}\n    73\tInfluence curves formalize local sensitivity \\citep{Hampel1974}, while observation diagnostics connect data perturbations to fitted parameters \\citep{Cook1977}. Influence functions have also been used to trace predictions to training observations \\citep{Koh2017}. The infinitesimal jackknife approximates weighted refitting \\citep{Giordano2019}, and finite-sample removal robustness explicitly asks whether dropping a small subset changes a conclusion \\citep{Broderick2020}. We use influence only to define and screen constrained deletion prefixes. Retained-data fitting evaluates those prefixes; it does not make the prefix family exhaustive or establish approximation-error bounds for our selection criterion.\n    74\t\n    75\t\\paragraph{Evaluation and conditional uncertainty.}\n    76\tChatbot Arena motivates the preference-ranking setting \\citep{Chiang2024}. Vote-rigging research studies deliberate manipulation of that platform \\citep{Min2025}; here the intervention is deletion from a fixed observed sample, not the collection of new strategic votes. Basketball records provide a second source of outcomes \\citep{NBAdata}, not another language-model benchmark. Bootstrap methods distinguish the statistic being resampled from its reported uncertainty \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997}; the Bayesian bootstrap is a different weighting construction \\citep{Rubin1981}. Clustered and crossed-data resampling require attention to dependence assumptions \\citep{Field2007,Owen2007}. Our multinomial provenance-group weights are a conditional diagnostic, not a validated population uncertainty model for all dependencies in either dataset.\n    77\t\n    78\t\\section{Method}\n    79\t\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\n    80\tLet $D=\\{(a_i,b_i,y_i,u_i)\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ contain observed comparisons of $m$ participants. Here $y_i\\in\\{0,\\tfrac12,1\\}$ is the outcome for endpoint $a_i$, and $u_i$ is a stable row key used only for deterministic tie resolution. Define $x_i=e_{a_i}-e_{b_i}$, margin $z_i=x_i^\\top\\theta$, and loss $\\ell_i(\\theta)=\\log(1+\\exp z_i)-y_i z_i$. The clean fit is\n    81\t\\begin{equation}\n    82\t \\widehat\\theta=\\arg\\min_{\\mathbf{1}^\\top\\theta=0}\n    83\t \\left\\{L_D(\\theta)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i\\in D}\\ell_i(\\theta)\n    84\t             +\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\theta\\|_2^2\\right\\},\\qquad \\lambda=0.001.\n    85\t \\label{eq:objective}\n    86\t\\end{equation}\n    87\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L29]]\n    88\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L653]]\n    89\tHalf-win outcomes use the same logistic cross-entropy, rather than an additional tie-probability parameter. A retained subset is fitted using its \\emph{own} mean loss and the same $\\lambda$. Consequently, deletion changes the likelihood contribution relative to the penalty; it is not equivalent to keeping a fixed sum-loss penalty.\n    90\t\n    91\tLet $\\pi(\\theta)$ rank scores in descending order, with rounded-score ties broken by participant identity. For clean leader $l$, the primary endpoint is normalized pairwise inversion:\n    92\t\\begin{equation}\n    93\t I(D,R)=\\frac{1}{\\binom m2}\\sum_{a<b}\n    94\t \\mathbf{1}\\!\\left\\{a,b\\text{ have opposite orders in }\n    95\t       \\pi(\\widehat\\theta_D),\\pi(\\widehat\\theta_{D\\setminus R})\\right\\}.\n    96\t \\label{eq:inversions}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tThe secondary endpoint is the clean leader's new zero-based rank divided by $m-1$. Both describe fitted-ranking disruption; neither evaluates predictive accuracy. Budgets are $k_f=\\lfloor fn\\rfloor$ for $f\\in\\{0.001,0.005,0.01\\}$. Equal row fractions provide a common sample-mass scale, but do not equalize participant exposure, graph topology, information loss, or difficulty across datasets.\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L680]]\n   100\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases]]\n   101\t\n   102\t\\subsection{Rationale: local orders, retained-data decisions}\n   103\tThe design rationale is to use local sensitivity for a tractable family of deletions while measuring finite deletions by refitting. For every clean nonleader $c$, form $v_c=e_l-e_c$ and solve $Hd_c=v_c$, where $H=\\nabla^2 L_D(\\widehat\\theta)$. The row influence for this contrast is\n   104\t\\begin{equation}\n   105\t q_{ic}=(\\sigmoid(x_i^\\top\\widehat\\theta)-y_i)x_i^\\top d_c.\n   106\t \\label{eq:influence}\n   107\t\\end{equation}\n   108\tAscending $q_{ic}$ gives an order $o_c$. The legal deletion at each budget is exactly $R_{cf}=o_c[1:k_f]$. The order is computed once from the clean fit and never rearranged after refitting. This restriction makes the output independently checkable, but excludes adaptive reorderings and arbitrary subset swaps.\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L145]]\n   110\t\n   111\tThe screening calculation predicts full-vector displacement using the clean Hessian. Writing $g_i=\\nabla\\ell_i(\\widehat\\theta)$, it uses\n   112\t\\begin{equation}\n   113\t \\widetilde\\delta_{cf}=\\frac{H^{-1}}{n-k_f}\n   114\t       \\left(\\sum_{i\\in R_{cf}}g_i+k_f\\lambda\\widehat\\theta\\right).\n   115\t \\label{eq:screen}\n   116\t\\end{equation}\n   117\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L181]]\n   118\tThe penalty correction follows from the retained-mean objective, not an extra intervention. The retained gradient at the clean optimum is $-(\\sum_{R}g_i+k\\lambda\\widehat\\theta)/(n-k)$; replacing retained curvature by $H$ gives Eq.~\\ref{eq:screen}. The approximation can misorder contenders for finite deletions. Refitting the shortlisted prefixes addresses that particular approximation in the decision, but does not remove the shortlist restriction.\n   119\t\n   120\t\\subsection{Compared approaches and selection rule}\n   121\t\\Exact{} screens every nonleader by a lexicographic tuple: the sum of predicted leader-rank drops across budgets; the number of target-gap reversals; summed clipped relative gap closure; and preference for a better clean rank. A shortlist contains at most six contenders, retaining the screening maximizer and the clean runner-up. Each shortlisted contender's frozen prefixes are refitted under Eq.~\\ref{eq:objective}, and the same tuple is evaluated using fitted, rather than predicted, scores. A reversal requires a leader--contender gap below $-10^{-8}$; relative closure is clipped to $[0,2]$. Incomplete refit sets are excluded. If none completes, selection falls back to the screening maximizer.\n   122\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L138]]\n   123\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L231]]\n   124\t\n   125\tThis selection rule targets leader demotion before other criteria, whereas the primary endpoint counts inversions anywhere in the list. Thus the method does not directly maximize Eq.~\\ref{eq:inversions}. High-loss deletion instead sorts descending clean per-row logistic loss, using stable keys for ties. Random deletion uses one seeded permutation and its nested prefixes. These comparators use the same row budgets and independent retained-data solver. A no-deletion check refits the unchanged data; it is a numerical control, not a competing attack.\n   126\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L949]]\n   127\t\n   128\tWe also retain descriptive development scores for clean-Hessian-only selection and a retained-curvature shortlist expansion in Supplementary Information. They are not controlled final-partition ablations. The final evidence evaluates the selected six-contender configuration against deletion baselines, not an isolated causal effect of exact-refit selection or extra curvature information.\n   129\t\n   130\t\\subsection{Implementation and independent verification}\n   131\tThe selector aggregates comparison counts and fractional wins by ordered endpoint pair. It uses centered Newton updates, Cholesky linear solves, a backtracking line search, warm starts across nested retained datasets, and a cache keyed by the removed row set. The independent evaluator instead starts L-BFGS-B fits from zero. It checks clean-score stationarity, the Hessian contrast solve, row influence values, and exact prefix membership before computing disruption itself. Candidate-authored diagnostics are not used as measured evidence.\n   132\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selector.py#L70]]\n   133\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L667]]\n   134\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L706]]\n   135\t\n   136\tReadable implementations accompany this paper: \\path{source_evidence/selector.py}, lines 70--263, defines fitting and selection; \\path{source_evidence/evaluate_final.py}, lines 647--731 and 831--871, defines fitting checks, disruption, and conditional uncertainty. The supplementary record states numerical tolerances and data-processing constraints. These source locations substantiate the mechanism; a source hash or an acceptance verdict alone would not do so.\n   137\t\n   138\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n   139\t\\subsection{Observed datasets and scope}\n   140\tArena uses the pinned public human-preference snapshot associated with the 55k release, not the larger optional release. NBA uses the pinned FiveThirtyEight historical outcome archive. The fitting targets are observed preference outcomes and game results, respectively; NBA's supplied Elo columns are not the response or fitted estimator. The final retained comparison graphs contain 64 Arena model identities and 74 NBA historical team codes. Stable identities are partition-specific: basketball codes need not correspond to persistent franchises or contemporaneous opponents.\n   141\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases]]\n   142\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n   143\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json]]\n   144\t\n   145\tThe preprocessing record groups shared normalized content before assigning development and final partitions. For Arena this includes shared prompt or response turns; for NBA it uses normalized game content and removes duplicate game-side copies. Each partition retains its largest undirected connected comparison component. This controls the declared cross-partition content overlap, not every source of statistical dependence. In particular, shared participants, annotator effects, temporal changes, and heterogeneous sports eras remain possible dependencies or modeling limitations.\n   146\t\n   147\tThere are no additional evaluated task families in the supplied final record. Expert-annotated judgments and model-judge comparisons are not measured here. The cross-setting comparison therefore asks whether a common audit behaves similarly on these specific datasets, not whether a language-model method transfers to a new predictive task or whether one evaluation design is intrinsically more stable.\n   148\t\n   149\t\\subsection{Budgets, repetitions, and reporting}\n   150\tAll recorded budgets and settings are reported without selecting only favorable cells. Floored deletion counts are 38, 190, and 381 for Arena, and 41, 207, and 414 for NBA. The final evaluation repeats the common comparison under five seeds, also reversing input row order as an invariance check. Reversed cases do not add independent datasets or extra deletion samples. The studied and high-loss curves are identical across recorded seeds and row-order controls; random deletion supplies the varying masks.\n   151\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   152\t\n   153\tAggregate disruption averages budgets and datasets equally, with the repeated row-order checks contributing identical values. This is an audit-level summary, not a row-weighted population effect. Incomplete or invalid execution is not silently removed: the evaluator has explicit failure handling and numerical checks. The authoritative record reports a completed evaluation meeting its acceptance checks. That outcome establishes neither out-of-sample generalization nor universal superiority of the deletion rule.\n   154\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n   155\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n   156\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.correct]]\n   157\t\n   158\t\\subsection{Two distinct uncertainty questions}\n   159\tSeed resampling summarizes variability due to the recorded randomized deletion masks. The evaluator resamples the five seed-level aggregate gains and records percentile endpoints. This interval does not incorporate new data, alternative preprocessing, or selecting the method from development comparisons. When both contrasted rules are deterministic, a degenerate seed interval is expected and must not be read as certainty about their broader ordering.\n   160\t\n   161\tThe conditional group-weight diagnostic asks a different question. For each dataset, multinomial draws over its provenance groups produce nonnegative row weights, constant within a group. The clean weighted ranking and the retained weighted rankings are refitted. The stored deletion masks---including a single seeded random mask family---are held fixed; contender selection and row ordering are not rerun. Each draw reports the mean inversion difference across budgets. Weighted removed mass can vary despite fixed nominal row counts. There are 32 unique stored draws per dataset, copied identically into each seed export; we count these once, not as independent repetitions across exports.\n   162\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L831]]\n   163\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   164\t\n   165\t\\section{Results}\n   166\t\\subsection{Aggregate disruption does not imply uniform dominance}\n   167\tAcross the recorded final comparison, \\Exact{} produces mean normalized inversions of 0.011722, against 0.009340 for high-loss deletion and 0.003903 for random deletion. These correspond to 1.172\\%, 0.934\\%, and 0.390\\% of participant pairs. The paired aggregate advantages are 0.238 and 0.782 percentage points, respectively. These are measured differences in disruption, not improvements in ranking accuracy. The studied selector's secondary mean normalized leader-rank drop is 0.033051; without a reported comparator breakdown for this endpoint, it should not be interpreted as a comparative leader-demotion advantage.\n   168\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n   169\t\n   170\tTable~\\ref{tab:budgets} and Figure~\\ref{fig:budgets} expose the heterogeneity hidden by the aggregate. At the smallest Arena budget, the studied selector inverts 0.595\\% of pairs, compared with 0.149\\% for high-loss deletion. At the largest budget the ordering reverses: 0.893\\% versus 1.637\\%. The intermediate Arena budget also favors high-loss deletion. On NBA, the studied selector is more disruptive than high-loss deletion at every measured budget, reaching 2.295\\% versus 1.666\\% at the largest. It also exceeds the mean random-deletion disruption in each reported dataset--budget cell. The last statement concerns the recorded mean, not every possible random mask.\n   171\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   172\t\n   173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   174\t \\centering\n   175\t \\input{results_table.tex}\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final evaluation completed and was qualified; this does not establish generalization or universal method superiority.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final mean inversion fractions are 0.011722185737596697 for the studied selector, 0.009340217174121283 for high-loss deletion, and 0.003902759168170127 for random deletion (1.172%, 0.934%, and 0.390% after rounding).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.combined_score\", \"final.metric.disagreement_baseline_score\", \"final.metric.random_baseline_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final aggregate paired disruption gains are 0.002381968563475412 against high-loss and 0.00781942656942657 against random deletion, or 0.238 and 0.782 percentage points rounded.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.paired_gain_vs_disagreement\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_vs_random\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The studied selector's aggregate normalized original-leader rank drop is 0.03305066318764949; no comparator-specific leader-drop result is claimed.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.mean_leader_rank_drop\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final evidence covers Arena and NBA at three deletion budgets with five seeds and reversed-row controls, not additional expert- or model-judge tasks.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes[*]/solution/cases\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final clean-score vectors have 64 Arena and 74 NBA participants. Prefix lengths are Arena 38/190/381 and NBA 41/207/414.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[0].solution.cases: clean_scores lengths and candidate_masks lengths\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"At Arena budgets 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, studied inversions are 0.005952380952380952/0.008432539682539682/0.008928571428571428; high-loss inversions are 0.001488095238095238/0.010416666666666666/0.01636904761904762. The high-loss comparator is more disruptive at the two larger budgets.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[*].solution.cases[family=arena,permuted=false].curves\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"At NBA budgets 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, studied inversions are 0.011477230655312847/0.012587930396149574/0.022954461310625694; high-loss inversions are 0.0018511662347278786/0.009255831173639394/0.016660496112550906. The studied selector is more disruptive at all measured NBA budgets.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[*].solution.cases[family=nba,permuted=false].curves\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The random-curve means in Table 1 and Figure 2 are computed over the five unpermuted seed cases per dataset; the studied selector exceeds those means in every dataset-budget cell.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"derived_artifacts\": [\"analysis_summary.json\", \"results_table.tex\", \"figures/dataset_budget_comparison.pdf\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Studied and high-loss curves are deterministic across recorded seeds, all corresponding reversed-row method curves agree, and no-deletion curves are zero.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.control_pass_rate\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[*].solution.cases[*].curves and controls\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"There are 32 unique complete fixed-mask group-weight records per dataset; the same block is repeated identically in all five seed exports and is not counted as 160 independent draws.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[*].solution.conditional_group_bootstrap\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"All stored conditional high-loss-relative gains are negative in Arena and positive in NBA. Arena range/mean is [-0.7936507937,-0.2314814815]/-0.5042989418 pp; NBA range/mean is [0.2221399482,1.9498951006]/0.8056429717 pp.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[0].solution.conditional_group_bootstrap.{arena,nba}[*].gain_disagreement\",\n      \"derived_artifacts\": [\"analysis_summary.json\", \"bootstrap_table.tex\", \"figures/conditional_resampling_contrasts.pdf\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Stored conditional random-relative gains range from -0.2149470899 to 0.4960317460 pp on Arena and 0.5923731951 to 2.2954461311 pp on NBA. These are stored ranges, not confidence bounds.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"locator\": \"episodes[0].solution.conditional_group_bootstrap.{arena,nba}[*].gain_random\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The final seed-resampling gain interval is [0.007377907506332164,0.008168732424939618] versus random; the high-loss-relative endpoints both equal 0.002381968563475412.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_low\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_random_ci_high\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_low\", \"final.metric.paired_gain_disagreement_ci_high\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Conditional group-weight uncertainty holds the first unpermuted masks fixed, refits weighted clean and deleted rankings, and does not repeat contender selection; weighted removed mass is not fixed by nominal row fraction.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Efron1979\", \"Davison1997\", \"Field2007\", \"Owen2007\"],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:831\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The evaluated objective is retained-mean logistic loss with centered scores, half-win ties, and quadratic ridge 0.001. All-pairs inversions and normalized leader drop are distinct endpoints.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Bradley1952\", \"Davidson1970\", \"Kendall1938\"],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:647\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:680\", \"source_evidence/selector.py:84\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Clean-Hessian contrast influence defines immutable nested prefixes at fractions 0.001,0.005,0.01. The screening displacement includes the retained-mean regularization correction in Equation 4.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Hampel1974\", \"Koh2017\", \"Giordano2019\", \"Broderick2020\"],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/selector.py:145\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:706\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Selection shortlists at most six contenders, retaining the screening maximizer and runner-up, compares exact retained fits by leader drops, target reversals below -1e-8, closure clipped to [0,2], and clean rank. Failed finalist fits are excluded, with fallback to the screening maximizer.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/selector.py:138\", \"source_evidence/selector.py:145\", \"source_evidence/selector.py:231\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The selected implementation uses ordered-pair sufficient statistics, centered Newton updates, Cholesky solves, line search, warm starts and removed-set caching. The independent evaluator fits from zero with L-BFGS-B.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/selector.py:70\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:667\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"High-loss deletion orders descending clean logistic loss with stable-key tie resolution; random deletion uses seeded permutation prefixes and all deletion methods share budgets and independent refitting.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:949\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Source-defined numerical settings, rounding rules, stationarity, contrast-solve, score-agreement and influence tolerances are those recorded in the supplementary methods; they are protocol settings, not performance measurements.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/selector.py:84\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:667\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:680\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:706\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The uncertainty source specifies seed 709237, 256 seed-mean resamples with .025/.975 quantiles, and 32 group-weight resamples per dataset; Figure 3 and the supplemental table retain the unique records only.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:831\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Partition metadata records pinned sources, normalized content grouping, deterministic content splits, largest components, and the cross-partition overlap audit. Final row counts are 38192 Arena and 41418 NBA, with NBA 2898 rows excluded outside its retained component; these are protocol metadata rather than comparative performance results.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Chiang2024\", \"NBAdata\"],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json\", \"source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json\", \"source_evidence/evaluate_final.py:769\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Descriptive development aggregate values are 0.013240276724865765 for clean-Hessian selection and 0.01595532053579999 for bounded exact refits and retained-curvature expansion. They are not final controlled ablations, equivalence tests, or runtime comparisons.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_03\", \"result.top_program_01\", \"result.top_program_02\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/clean_hessian_selector.py\", \"source_evidence/expanded_selector.py\", \"source_evidence/selector.py\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The retained-curvature comparison configuration uses at most 24 proxy contenders and 12 exact finalists, preserving the bounded selector finalists; no causal attribution of its score is made.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.top_program_02\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"implementation_sources\": [\"source_evidence/expanded_selector.py:153\", \"source_evidence/expanded_selector.py:266\", \"source_evidence/expanded_selector.py:312\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Classical ranking, estimation, influence and bootstrap references supply context, not measurements of the evaluated configuration.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"ref.Zermelo1929\", \"ref.Thurstone1927\", \"ref.Bradley1952\", \"ref.Ford1957\", \"ref.Hunter2004\", \"ref.Turner2012\", \"ref.Davidson1970\", \"ref.Firth1993\", \"ref.Plackett1975\", \"ref.Negahban2012\", \"ref.Shah2017\", \"ref.Kendall1938\", \"ref.Hampel1974\", \"ref.Cook1977\", \"ref.Koh2017\", \"ref.Giordano2019\", \"ref.Broderick2020\", \"ref.Chiang2024\", \"ref.Min2025\", \"ref.NBAdata\", \"ref.Efron1979\", \"ref.Davison1997\", \"ref.Rubin1981\", \"ref.Field2007\", \"ref.Owen2007\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"Zermelo1929\", \"Thurstone1927\", \"Bradley1952\", \"Ford1957\", \"Hunter2004\", \"Turner2012\", \"Davidson1970\", \"Firth1993\", \"Plackett1975\", \"Negahban2012\", \"Shah2017\", \"Kendall1938\", \"Hampel1974\", \"Cook1977\", \"Koh2017\", \"Giordano2019\", \"Broderick2020\", \"Chiang2024\", \"Min2025\", \"NBAdata\", \"Efron1979\", \"Davison1997\", \"Rubin1981\", \"Field2007\", \"Owen2007\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
