{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '170,238p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '269,365p'\"","aggregated_output":"   170\tTable~\\ref{tab:budgets} and Figure~\\ref{fig:budgets} expose the heterogeneity hidden by the aggregate. At the smallest Arena budget, the studied selector inverts 0.595\\% of pairs, compared with 0.149\\% for high-loss deletion. At the largest budget the ordering reverses: 0.893\\% versus 1.637\\%. The intermediate Arena budget also favors high-loss deletion. On NBA, the studied selector is more disruptive than high-loss deletion at every measured budget, reaching 2.295\\% versus 1.666\\% at the largest. It also exceeds the mean random-deletion disruption in each reported dataset--budget cell. The last statement concerns the recorded mean, not every possible random mask.\n   171\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   172\t\n   173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   174\t \\centering\n   175\t \\input{results_table.tex}\n   176\t \\caption{Inverted participant pairs (\\%) on final partitions. Removed-row counts are floored at the nominal 0.1\\%, 0.5\\%, and 1\\% budgets. Exact-refit and high-loss values are deterministic in the recorded evaluations; random entries average the five seeds. Greater inversion means greater sensitivity, not better estimation.}\n   177\t \\label{tab:budgets}\n   178\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   179\t\\end{table}\n   180\t\n   181\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   182\t \\centering\n   183\t \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/dataset_budget_comparison.pdf}\n   184\t \\caption{Budget-resolved disruption on (a) Arena and (b) NBA, with a shared vertical scale. Solid markers show deterministic values or random-mask means; faint random markers show the five seeds, not uncertainty bounds. Removed-row counts are Arena: 38/190/381; NBA: 41/207/414. Input-order controls are not plotted as additional replicates.}\n   185\t \\label{fig:budgets}\n   186\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   187\t\\end{figure}\n   188\t\n   189\tThe measured contrast is consistent with a restricted interpretation of the selector. Its ranking of contenders prioritizes the clean leader, while high-loss deletion can perturb other parts of the order. That mismatch makes uniform superiority on all-pairs inversions unnecessary. It does not, however, explain the observed dataset difference causally: the exports do not contain a controlled decomposition of which criterion, contender, or graph property accounts for the difference. Nor does the response to these budgets identify the smallest deletion sufficient for any specific reversal.\n   190\t\n   191\t\\subsection{Fixed-mask resampling preserves a cross-setting contrast}\n   192\tFigure~\\ref{fig:conditional} displays every unique stored conditional gain. All high-loss-relative gains are negative on Arena and positive on NBA. In percentage-point units, the Arena gains range from $-0.794$ to $-0.231$, with mean $-0.504$; the NBA gains range from $0.222$ to $1.950$, with mean $0.806$. The aggregate advantage therefore cannot be promoted to an unconditional statement that this selector is a stronger deletion rule on both datasets. Under the documented reweighting, the relative disruption remains setting-dependent.\n   193\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   194\t\n   195\tRelative to fixed random masks, Arena gains include negative values, ranging from $-0.215$ to $0.496$ percentage points. NBA random-relative gains are positive throughout the stored records, ranging from $0.592$ to $2.295$ percentage points. These ranges describe the recorded draws, not confidence limits or a probability of future superiority. All stored draws completed their weighted refits; duplicated exports add no additional evidence about that variability.\n   196\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   197\t\n   198\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   199\t \\centering\n   200\t \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/conditional_resampling_contrasts.pdf}\n   201\t \\caption{Conditional differences in mean inversion disruption, exact-refit minus (a) high-loss or (b) random deletion. Each dataset contributes its 32 unique group-weight draws; horizontal jitter only separates points. Dataset colors have the same meaning in both panels. The zero line denotes equal disruption. Masks remain fixed, weighted removed mass varies, and no selection is repeated. These are not full-procedure confidence intervals.}\n   202\t \\label{fig:conditional}\n   203\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   204\t\\end{figure}\n   205\t\n   206\tThe seed-resampling endpoints address a narrower source of randomness. For the aggregate gain over random deletion, they are $[0.007378,0.008169]$ in inversion-fraction units. For the high-loss-relative gain, both endpoints equal 0.002382 because the compared curves are deterministic across seeds. This equality is not evidence of negligible data uncertainty: the conditional reweighting results visibly change the gains and expose opposite dataset orderings. Conversely, the conditional draws do not supply uncertainty for a deletion rule that could adapt its masks after reweighting.\n   207\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_random_ci_low]]\n   208\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_random_ci_high]]\n   209\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_disagreement_ci_low]]\n   210\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_disagreement_ci_high]]\n   211\t\n   212\t\\subsection{Numerical checks and configuration evidence}\n   213\tThe no-deletion curves have zero disruption, and reversing input order leaves every recorded method curve unchanged within its seed and dataset. These checks support a numerical and indexing interpretation of the reported comparisons rather than an artifact of row order or refitting unchanged data. They do not establish completeness of the deletion search, realism of an adversary, or adequacy of the statistical model. The source-defined certificate checks were satisfied in the completed final evaluation; candidate-generated auxiliary descriptions remain unverified.\n   214\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   215\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n   216\t\n   217\tDevelopment configurations are informative only as descriptive context. They share influence-prefix constraints but differ in how contenders are screened or refitted. A tied development aggregate for the bounded exact-refit and retained-curvature configurations does not show identical masks, equal runtime, or equivalence on the final partition. No final controlled ablation estimates the effect of replacing the shortlist rule while holding the rest of the evaluated procedure fixed. We therefore attribute the final measurements to the evaluated configuration as a whole.\n   218\t\n   219\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   220\t\\paragraph{What the audit establishes.}\n   221\tA deletion witness demonstrates that a particular fitted ranking is dependent on a particular subset of observed outcomes under a stated objective. Independent refitting makes that finite perturbation observable. Normalization by the number of participant pairs helps compare lists of different lengths, but assigns equal weight to all pair inversions, including changes far from the top. A ranking with a small global inversion fraction can still change an important local comparison. The secondary leader metric partly addresses this issue, without supplying a complete account of top-rank uncertainty.\n   222\t\n   223\t\\paragraph{What the comparison does not isolate.}\n   224\tArena and NBA differ in participants, comparison density, outcomes, content dependence, time span, and preprocessing. Their different responses cannot be attributed to crowd voting versus sports competition alone. The historical team-code model is static across eras, and the largest-component filter changes which participants are included. Equal row fractions do not remove these confounds. No matched-support intervention, expert-versus-model-judge study, or graph-property ablation is available. The scientific conclusion is conditional on these retained graphs, budgets, objective, and deletion rules.\n   225\t\n   226\t\\paragraph{Restrictions of selection and uncertainty.}\n   227\tThe contender shortlist is bounded; within-contender orders remain fixed; selection favors leader demotion rather than the reported all-pairs objective. A weak result can arise from any of these restrictions, and the present evidence does not separate them. The group-weight diagnostic respects declared content groups, not all possible crossed dependence. It holds masks fixed and uses a single stored random-mask family. It neither adjusts for development selection nor supplies uncertainty for attack reselection. No ridge-sensitivity experiment, full adaptive uncertainty procedure, or runtime comparison is reported.\n   228\t\n   229\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity is not a confidence statement.}\n   230\tThe deletion comparison and the uncertainty diagnostic have different reference objects. A deletion witness compares a retained fit with the original clean fit. A weighted draw instead compares weighted retained fits with a newly fitted weighted clean ranking. Thus a change in a conditional gain need not mean that the same participant pair crossed the same original boundary. Rank inversion is also discontinuous in fitted scores: a small score displacement near a tie can change the order, whereas a larger displacement away from a tie may not. The measured inversion fraction cannot by itself identify either the score margin or practical importance of the affected comparisons. Reporting the full budget-resolved curves together with their conditioning assumptions is more informative than treating an aggregate gain or a seed interval as a universal stability certificate.\n   231\t\n   232\t\\paragraph{Reproducibility and responsible interpretation.}\n   233\tThe supplied source snapshots, normalized resources, evidence exports, and figure-generation script support inspection and regeneration of the reported summaries. A complete raw-source-to-partition builder and the original raw archives are not included in the staged materials, so end-to-end reproduction from raw public data remains incomplete. Supplementary Information distinguishes this gap from reproducible figure extraction. The analysis is retrospective: it does not demonstrate access to live votes or practical manipulation of any deployed leaderboard. Results should motivate transparent reporting of sensitivity, not claims that a model's quality or a team's ability changed when observations were removed.\n   234\t\n   235\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   236\tThis study evaluates fitted-ranking sensitivity through constrained row deletion and independent refitting. Clean influence orders define checkable nested subsets, while bounded retained-data selection chooses among contender-specific prefixes. On the recorded final partitions, the evaluated configuration yields greater aggregate pairwise disruption than high-loss and random deletion. The more informative result is the lack of uniformity: high-loss deletion is more disruptive at the larger Arena budgets, whereas the studied selector exceeds it throughout the measured NBA budgets. Conditional group-weight resampling retains opposite high-loss-relative signs across these settings.\n   237\t\n   238\tThese findings support reporting sensitivity as a dataset-, budget-, and intervention-specific property. They do not identify a globally minimal perturbation, validate an unrestricted attack, or isolate a causal effect of evaluation design. A useful extension would preserve independent refitting while comparing matched supports, alternative ranking objectives, and a fully repeated selection procedure under dependence-aware resampling. Those extensions are not measured here. For current use, the warranted conclusion is narrower: an aggregate disruption advantage can coexist with meaningful comparator reversals and conditional uncertainty that should remain visible to readers.\n   269\t\\subsection{Data partitioning and source provenance}\n   270\tThe Arena snapshot is \\path{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-55k} at commit \\nolinkurl{18c298340948c0e7f7727399fd459cca6ce0ca6f}; its supplied raw CSV SHA-256 is \\nolinkurl{0692154aaf20fc6649090c3f49b6b5dd1e693a765ecb5f0856ae0da5946f5be2}. The NBA source is \\path{fivethirtyeight/data}, commit \\nolinkurl{4c1ff5e3aef1816ae04af63218015066e186c147}, file \\path{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, with SHA-256 \\nolinkurl{d46ed3540ee8d9eca31b3e94cc8c777e0be5156173d814ebf65b8195e8d616bc}. Source licenses are Apache-2.0 and CC-BY-4.0, respectively. These identifiers are provenance, not evidence that every processing detail can be reconstructed from the public files alone.\n   271\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n   272\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json]]\n   273\t\n   274\tThe recorded normalization applies NFKC and Unicode whitespace collapse to parsed text turns; missing values become empty strings and empty turns are conservatively grouped. Arena grouping connects rows sharing any normalized prompt or response turn or normalized raw example, before outcome extraction. NBA uses normalized date, sorted original team codes, and aligned scores, retaining only \\texttt{\\_iscopy=0}. Partition membership hashes the fixed split identifier, dataset family, and group; hash residue below 30 modulo 100 is assigned to development, with the complement final. Largest connected components are selected within each partition.\n   275\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n   276\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L769]]\n   277\t\n   278\tThe final manifests record 38,192 retained Arena rows and 41,418 retained NBA rows. NBA excludes 2,898 rows outside its retained component; Arena excludes none at this component-filtering step. Both manifests report zero cross-partition content and source-identity intersections. These checks concern the declared normalization and source identifiers, not unobserved dependence. Historical NBA identities are team codes rather than a harmonized franchise-by-era model. A manuscript-side source inventory and regeneration commands appear in \\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}.\n   279\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n   280\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json]]\n   281\t\n   282\t\\subsection{Numerical and input-order controls}\n   283\tRecorded no-deletion inversion curves are identically zero. Each seed contains Arena and NBA cases in canonical and reversed input order; corresponding method curves agree. The evaluator additionally checks rejection of a constant invalid certificate when the zero-score gradient is nonzero, verifies valid clean certificates, and enforces independent refits. Stored control records pass. The authoritative final record states that evaluation completed and its qualification outcome passed. This is a statement about the tested execution and acceptance checks, not a proof of unrestricted statistical validity, generalization, or causal mechanism.\n   284\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   285\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n   286\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n   287\t\n   288\t\\subsection{Descriptive development configurations}\n   289\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   290\t \\centering\n   291\t \\begin{tabular}{lr}\n   292\t \\toprule\n   293\t Configuration & Development disruption \\\\\n   294\t \\midrule\n   295\t Clean-Hessian selection & 0.0132403 \\\\\n   296\t Bounded exact-refit selection & 0.0159553 \\\\\n   297\t Retained-curvature expansion & 0.0159553 \\\\\n   298\t \\bottomrule\n   299\t \\end{tabular}\n   300\t \\caption{Descriptive development aggregates, separate from the final-partition results. These are not replicated final controlled ablations, and the tied aggregate does not imply equivalent behavior or equal computation.}\n   301\t \\label{tab:development}\n   302\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_03]]\n   303\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_01]]\n   304\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_02]]\n   305\t\\end{table}\n   306\tReadable snapshots \\path{source_evidence/clean_hessian_selector.py} and \\path{source_evidence/expanded_selector.py} document the comparison configurations. The former selects using clean-Hessian predictions without the bounded retained-data finalist comparison. The latter uses a retained-curvature proxy pool of at most 24 contenders and at most 12 exact finalists, preserving the bounded selector's finalists. This source evidence establishes the configured differences, not the reason for the recorded scores. Selection from development evidence can induce optimism; the final partition is reported separately, and no development-selection-adjusted interval is available.\n   307\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/clean_hessian_selector.py]]\n   308\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/expanded_selector.py]]\n   309\t\n   310\t\\subsection{Conditional uncertainty and complete stored contrasts}\n   311\tThe uncertainty routine uses random seed 709237. It draws 256 resamples of the five seed-level means to compute the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles reported in the main text. Separately, for each dataset it draws 32 multinomial provenance-group weight vectors and refits the clean and deleted weighted datasets. It uses the first unpermuted stored mask family, rather than pooling masks from all seeds. The same group-weight block is duplicated in each seed export; the table below contains only the unique block. Zero-weight observations remain represented through their weight, and weights on selected mask rows are set to zero before retained fitting. Normalization uses total retained weight.\n   312\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L831]]\n   313\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   314\t\n   315\t\\begin{table}[h]\n   316\t \\centering\n   317\t \\small\n   318\t % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/conditional_group_bootstrap]]\n   319\t \\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}\n   320\t \\toprule\n   321\t  & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Arena} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{NBA} \\\\\n   322\t Draw & vs. high-loss & vs. random & vs. high-loss & vs. random \\\\\n   323\t \\midrule\n   324\t 1 & -0.794 & +0.066 & +0.827 & +1.222 \\\\\n   325\t 2 & -0.661 & +0.265 & +0.666 & +1.123 \\\\\n   326\t 3 & -0.496 & +0.182 & +0.518 & +1.074 \\\\\n   327\t 4 & -0.331 & +0.066 & +1.049 & +1.283 \\\\\n   328\t 5 & -0.562 & +0.116 & +1.432 & +1.678 \\\\\n   329\t 6 & -0.529 & +0.000 & +1.432 & +1.728 \\\\\n   330\t 7 & -0.496 & +0.496 & +0.568 & +0.889 \\\\\n   331\t 8 & -0.380 & +0.347 & +0.432 & +0.901 \\\\\n   332\t 9 & -0.364 & +0.165 & +1.135 & +1.703 \\\\\n   333\t 10 & -0.231 & +0.132 & +0.642 & +0.963 \\\\\n   334\t 11 & -0.711 & -0.215 & +1.308 & +1.543 \\\\\n   335\t 12 & -0.513 & +0.182 & +0.247 & +0.654 \\\\\n   336\t 13 & -0.413 & +0.182 & +1.061 & +1.049 \\\\\n   337\t 14 & -0.529 & +0.033 & +0.827 & +1.234 \\\\\n   338\t 15 & -0.661 & +0.265 & +0.222 & +0.716 \\\\\n   339\t 16 & -0.446 & +0.198 & +0.383 & +0.592 \\\\\n   340\t 17 & -0.380 & +0.132 & +0.950 & +1.358 \\\\\n   341\t 18 & -0.281 & +0.083 & +0.864 & +1.172 \\\\\n   342\t 19 & -0.777 & +0.198 & +0.913 & +1.074 \\\\\n   343\t 20 & -0.479 & -0.066 & +0.481 & +0.815 \\\\\n   344\t 21 & -0.645 & +0.099 & +0.358 & +1.049 \\\\\n   345\t 22 & -0.331 & -0.083 & +0.827 & +1.185 \\\\\n   346\t 23 & -0.430 & -0.165 & +0.975 & +1.259 \\\\\n   347\t 24 & -0.513 & +0.165 & +0.777 & +1.296 \\\\\n   348\t 25 & -0.744 & +0.132 & +0.617 & +0.864 \\\\\n   349\t 26 & -0.529 & +0.248 & +0.642 & +0.728 \\\\\n   350\t 27 & -0.529 & -0.066 & +0.950 & +1.320 \\\\\n   351\t 28 & -0.347 & +0.248 & +0.790 & +0.913 \\\\\n   352\t 29 & -0.347 & +0.132 & +1.000 & +1.370 \\\\\n   353\t 30 & -0.595 & +0.231 & +0.506 & +1.049 \\\\\n   354\t 31 & -0.645 & +0.215 & +0.432 & +0.987 \\\\\n   355\t 32 & -0.446 & +0.132 & +1.950 & +2.295 \\\\\n   356\t \\bottomrule\n   357\t \\end{tabular}\n   358\t \\caption{All unique stored conditional gain records in percentage points. Positive values mean greater disruption for the studied selector. Draw indices are identifiers, not additional experimental conditions. These fixed-mask weighted refits do not estimate uncertainty for reselection.}\n   359\t \\label{tab:bootstrap}\n   360\t\\end{table}\n   361\t\n   362\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n   363\tLLM assistance was used for research-question development, generation and revision of candidate analysis code, reference-query planning, source inspection, manuscript drafting, and manuscript-side plotting and validation code. Numerical comparisons reported here originate from the supplied evaluation artifacts, not from LLM predictions or invented measurements. The paper-writing process did not rerun the scientific evaluation or add new experimental conditions. Reference records and available abstracts were consulted using exact identities; citation traversal and broad leaderboard-deletion searches were not used. Model identities and human oversight not established by the supplied process record are not inferred. The incomplete raw-data reconstruction path remains a limitation despite automated assistance.\n   364\t\n   365\t\\end{document}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '206,238p' paper.tex; sed -n '269,310p' paper.tex; jq 'keys, (.final_evaluation|keys), (.downstream_study // null)' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '206,238p' paper.tex; sed -n '269,310p' paper.tex; jq 'keys, (.final_evaluation|keys), (.downstream_study // null)' paper_data.json\"","aggregated_output":"The seed-resampling endpoints address a narrower source of randomness. For the aggregate gain over random deletion, they are $[0.007378,0.008169]$ in inversion-fraction units. For the high-loss-relative gain, both endpoints equal 0.002382 because the compared curves are deterministic across seeds. This equality is not evidence of negligible data uncertainty: the conditional reweighting results visibly change the gains and expose opposite dataset orderings. Conversely, the conditional draws do not supply uncertainty for a deletion rule that could adapt its masks after reweighting.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_random_ci_low]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_random_ci_high]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_disagreement_ci_low]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/paired_gain_disagreement_ci_high]]\n\n\\subsection{Numerical checks and configuration evidence}\nThe no-deletion curves have zero disruption, and reversing input order leaves every recorded method curve unchanged within its seed and dataset. These checks support a numerical and indexing interpretation of the reported comparisons rather than an artifact of row order or refitting unchanged data. They do not establish completeness of the deletion search, realism of an adversary, or adequacy of the statistical model. The source-defined certificate checks were satisfied in the completed final evaluation; candidate-generated auxiliary descriptions remain unverified.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n\nDevelopment configurations are informative only as descriptive context. They share influence-prefix constraints but differ in how contenders are screened or refitted. A tied development aggregate for the bounded exact-refit and retained-curvature configurations does not show identical masks, equal runtime, or equivalence on the final partition. No final controlled ablation estimates the effect of replacing the shortlist rule while holding the rest of the evaluated procedure fixed. We therefore attribute the final measurements to the evaluated configuration as a whole.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the audit establishes.}\nA deletion witness demonstrates that a particular fitted ranking is dependent on a particular subset of observed outcomes under a stated objective. Independent refitting makes that finite perturbation observable. Normalization by the number of participant pairs helps compare lists of different lengths, but assigns equal weight to all pair inversions, including changes far from the top. A ranking with a small global inversion fraction can still change an important local comparison. The secondary leader metric partly addresses this issue, without supplying a complete account of top-rank uncertainty.\n\n\\paragraph{What the comparison does not isolate.}\nArena and NBA differ in participants, comparison density, outcomes, content dependence, time span, and preprocessing. Their different responses cannot be attributed to crowd voting versus sports competition alone. The historical team-code model is static across eras, and the largest-component filter changes which participants are included. Equal row fractions do not remove these confounds. No matched-support intervention, expert-versus-model-judge study, or graph-property ablation is available. The scientific conclusion is conditional on these retained graphs, budgets, objective, and deletion rules.\n\n\\paragraph{Restrictions of selection and uncertainty.}\nThe contender shortlist is bounded; within-contender orders remain fixed; selection favors leader demotion rather than the reported all-pairs objective. A weak result can arise from any of these restrictions, and the present evidence does not separate them. The group-weight diagnostic respects declared content groups, not all possible crossed dependence. It holds masks fixed and uses a single stored random-mask family. It neither adjusts for development selection nor supplies uncertainty for attack reselection. No ridge-sensitivity experiment, full adaptive uncertainty procedure, or runtime comparison is reported.\n\n\\paragraph{Sensitivity is not a confidence statement.}\nThe deletion comparison and the uncertainty diagnostic have different reference objects. A deletion witness compares a retained fit with the original clean fit. A weighted draw instead compares weighted retained fits with a newly fitted weighted clean ranking. Thus a change in a conditional gain need not mean that the same participant pair crossed the same original boundary. Rank inversion is also discontinuous in fitted scores: a small score displacement near a tie can change the order, whereas a larger displacement away from a tie may not. The measured inversion fraction cannot by itself identify either the score margin or practical importance of the affected comparisons. Reporting the full budget-resolved curves together with their conditioning assumptions is more informative than treating an aggregate gain or a seed interval as a universal stability certificate.\n\n\\paragraph{Reproducibility and responsible interpretation.}\nThe supplied source snapshots, normalized resources, evidence exports, and figure-generation script support inspection and regeneration of the reported summaries. A complete raw-source-to-partition builder and the original raw archives are not included in the staged materials, so end-to-end reproduction from raw public data remains incomplete. Supplementary Information distinguishes this gap from reproducible figure extraction. The analysis is retrospective: it does not demonstrate access to live votes or practical manipulation of any deployed leaderboard. Results should motivate transparent reporting of sensitivity, not claims that a model's quality or a team's ability changed when observations were removed.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nThis study evaluates fitted-ranking sensitivity through constrained row deletion and independent refitting. Clean influence orders define checkable nested subsets, while bounded retained-data selection chooses among contender-specific prefixes. On the recorded final partitions, the evaluated configuration yields greater aggregate pairwise disruption than high-loss and random deletion. The more informative result is the lack of uniformity: high-loss deletion is more disruptive at the larger Arena budgets, whereas the studied selector exceeds it throughout the measured NBA budgets. Conditional group-weight resampling retains opposite high-loss-relative signs across these settings.\n\nThese findings support reporting sensitivity as a dataset-, budget-, and intervention-specific property. They do not identify a globally minimal perturbation, validate an unrestricted attack, or isolate a causal effect of evaluation design. A useful extension would preserve independent refitting while comparing matched supports, alternative ranking objectives, and a fully repeated selection procedure under dependence-aware resampling. Those extensions are not measured here. For current use, the warranted conclusion is narrower: an aggregate disruption advantage can coexist with meaningful comparator reversals and conditional uncertainty that should remain visible to readers.\n\\subsection{Data partitioning and source provenance}\nThe Arena snapshot is \\path{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-55k} at commit \\nolinkurl{18c298340948c0e7f7727399fd459cca6ce0ca6f}; its supplied raw CSV SHA-256 is \\nolinkurl{0692154aaf20fc6649090c3f49b6b5dd1e693a765ecb5f0856ae0da5946f5be2}. The NBA source is \\path{fivethirtyeight/data}, commit \\nolinkurl{4c1ff5e3aef1816ae04af63218015066e186c147}, file \\path{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, with SHA-256 \\nolinkurl{d46ed3540ee8d9eca31b3e94cc8c777e0be5156173d814ebf65b8195e8d616bc}. Source licenses are Apache-2.0 and CC-BY-4.0, respectively. These identifiers are provenance, not evidence that every processing detail can be reconstructed from the public files alone.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json]]\n\nThe recorded normalization applies NFKC and Unicode whitespace collapse to parsed text turns; missing values become empty strings and empty turns are conservatively grouped. Arena grouping connects rows sharing any normalized prompt or response turn or normalized raw example, before outcome extraction. NBA uses normalized date, sorted original team codes, and aligned scores, retaining only \\texttt{\\_iscopy=0}. Partition membership hashes the fixed split identifier, dataset family, and group; hash residue below 30 modulo 100 is assigned to development, with the complement final. Largest connected components are selected within each partition.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L769]]\n\nThe final manifests record 38,192 retained Arena rows and 41,418 retained NBA rows. NBA excludes 2,898 rows outside its retained component; Arena excludes none at this component-filtering step. Both manifests report zero cross-partition content and source-identity intersections. These checks concern the declared normalization and source identifiers, not unobserved dependence. Historical NBA identities are team codes rather than a harmonized franchise-by-era model. A manuscript-side source inventory and regeneration commands appear in \\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/arena_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/nba_manifest.json]]\n\n\\subsection{Numerical and input-order controls}\nRecorded no-deletion inversion curves are identically zero. Each seed contains Arena and NBA cases in canonical and reversed input order; corresponding method curves agree. The evaluator additionally checks rejection of a constant invalid certificate when the zero-score gradient is nonzero, verifies valid clean certificates, and enforces independent refits. Stored control records pass. The authoritative final record states that evaluation completed and its qualification outcome passed. This is a statement about the tested execution and acceptance checks, not a proof of unrestricted statistical validity, generalization, or causal mechanism.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Descriptive development configurations}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n \\centering\n \\begin{tabular}{lr}\n \\toprule\n Configuration & Development disruption \\\\\n \\midrule\n Clean-Hessian selection & 0.0132403 \\\\\n Bounded exact-refit selection & 0.0159553 \\\\\n Retained-curvature expansion & 0.0159553 \\\\\n \\bottomrule\n \\end{tabular}\n \\caption{Descriptive development aggregates, separate from the final-partition results. These are not replicated final controlled ablations, and the tied aggregate does not imply equivalent behavior or equal computation.}\n \\label{tab:development}\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_03]]\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_01]]\n % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.top_program_02]]\n\\end{table}\nReadable snapshots \\path{source_evidence/clean_hessian_selector.py} and \\path{source_evidence/expanded_selector.py} document the comparison configurations. The former selects using clean-Hessian predictions without the bounded retained-data finalist comparison. The latter uses a retained-curvature proxy pool of at most 24 contenders and at most 12 exact finalists, preserving the bounded selector's finalists. This source evidence establishes the configured differences, not the reason for the recorded scores. Selection from development evidence can induce optimism; the final partition is reported separately, and no development-selection-adjusted interval is available.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/clean_hessian_selector.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/expanded_selector.py]]\n\n\\subsection{Conditional uncertainty and complete stored contrasts}\n[\n  \"ablation_studies\",\n  \"all_programs\",\n  \"best_any\",\n  \"best_correct\",\n  \"evidence_sources\",\n  \"final_evaluation\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"grounding_sources\",\n  \"idea_titles\",\n  \"improvement_trace\",\n  \"paper_profile\",\n  \"presentation_contract\",\n  \"results_dir\",\n  \"top_programs\",\n  \"totals\"\n]\n[\n  \"correct\",\n  \"evaluation_evidence\",\n  \"evaluator_sha256\",\n  \"manifest_path\",\n  \"metrics\",\n  \"outcome\",\n  \"program_tree_sha256\",\n  \"results_tree_sha256\",\n  \"status\",\n  \"task_tree_sha256\"\n]\nnull\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
